                <h2>Why Your Ads Run But Don't Convert</h2>
<p>If you've been running Meta ads for any time, you've likely faced this frustrating scenario: your ad spends quickly, gets clicks, but generates zero conversions. This is the classic difference between ads that "run" versus ads that "sell."</p>
<p><strong>Running</strong>: The ad gets impressions, clicks, and the algorithm finds your target audience.</p>
<p><strong>Selling</strong>: The ad not only spends but drives actual conversions with positive ROI.</p>
<p>These two aren't the same. A "running" ad might have inflated CTR from clickbait creative, but users don't take action. A "selling" ad might struggle initially due to targeting or bidding, but once optimized, it becomes profitable. Below are six practical Q&amp;As to help you distinguish between them.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q1: Which Metrics Best Separate "Running" from "Selling" Ads?</h2>
<p><strong>Focus on three ratios:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric Combo</th>
<th>Running</th>
<th>Selling</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>CTR + CPA</td>
<td>High CTR, high CPA</td>
<td>Moderate CTR, low CPA</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPC + CVR</td>
<td>Low CPC, low CVR</td>
<td>Moderate CPC, CVR above average</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Spend + ROI</td>
<td>Fast spend, ROI &lt;1</td>
<td>Steady spend, ROI &gt;1.5</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Key insight</strong>: High CTR but low conversion rate = clickbait creative that attracts attention but fails to drive action. Classic "runs but doesn't sell."</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q2: When Should You Make the First Judgment Call on New Creatives?</h2>
<p><strong>Standard answer: Check impressions within 48 hours, conversions within 72 hours.</strong></p>
<p>Many advertisers only look at spend amount before drawing conclusions. Here's the right cadence:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>0-24 hours</strong>: Is the ad passing learning phase? Is the bid reasonable? Is there volume? No spend = ECPM too low or targeting too narrow.</li>
<li><strong>24-48 hours</strong>: Check CTR and CPC. High CTR but expensive CPC = creative works but faces stiff competition or landing page issues.</li>
<li><strong>48-72 hours</strong>: Check conversion cost and first purchase. No conversions at this stage means check landing page alignment or creative-to-landing consistency.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Don't wait a week to review.</strong> Wasted spend during cold start is hard to recover with later improvements.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q3: An Ad Spends But Doesn't Convert—Lower Bid or Kill It?</h2>
<p><strong>Diagnose first, decide second.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Scenario A: Low CTR, slow spend</strong><br />
This creative never really ran. Kill it directly.</p>
<p><strong>Scenario B: High CTR, fast spend, no conversions</strong><br />
This is the most common "runs but doesn't sell" pattern. Don't kill it immediately. Instead:<br />
1. Check landing page load speed and content match<br />
2. Compare against high-converting ads in the same campaign<br />
3. Try lowering bid or narrowing audience to filter for higher intent</p>
<p>If no conversion signal within 48 hours of adjustments, kill it. Running doesn't equal worth running.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q4: Why Do "Running" Ads Have Poor ROI?</h2>
<p><strong>Three common causes:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Creative-Audience Mismatch</strong><br />
A heavy discount-focused creative targeting price-insensitive premium users won't convert. Users click but leave at checkout.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Landing Page Disconnect</strong><br />
Promised features/benefits in the ad aren't immediately visible on landing page. Trust breaks, users leave. This is the #1 ROI killer in e-commerce campaigns.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Overly Aggressive Bidding</strong><br />
Raising bids to win volume brings traffic but at unsustainable CPA. Better to restart with proper bids than to bleed money.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Q5: How to Quickly Identify "Hidden Winners" vs. "Fake Runners"?</h2>
<p><strong>One technique: Look at mid-funnel user actions beyond conversions.</strong></p>
<p>In Meta Events Manager, check:<br />
- Add to Cart rate<br />
- Initiate Checkout rate</p>
<p>If your ad drives many add-to-cart but few checkouts → landing page conversion path is broken, creative quality is actually fine.</p>
<p>If your ad drives few add-to-cart → the problem is in the creative itself.</p>
<p><strong>Hidden winner signals</strong>: Moderate-to-good CTR + ATC/checkout rate above account average + steady (not explosive) spend growth.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q6: How to Budget Between "Running" and "Selling" Creatives?</h2>
<p><strong>Golden budget split: 60% to proven performers, 30% to optimization tests, 10% to new tests.</strong></p>
<p>In reality, many advertisers put 80% on old "running" creatives and starve new tests. Correct approach:</p>
<ul>
<li>Keep stable budget on "selling" creatives, watch for decay signals</li>
<li>Cap spend on "running but low ROI" creatives while testing adjustments</li>
<li>Always reserve budget for new creatives—even 10%—that's the fuel for account growth</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p><strong>A running ad isn't necessarily worth running. Only selling ads deserve scaling.</strong> When reviewing performance, focus on mid-funnel conversion data, not just impressions and clicks. Those metrics prove the ad moves, not that it makes you money.</p>
